1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,790
Your deployment is green. Then you roll back, and a new customer order disappears.

2
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:12,984
Built by Shivam Gupta, UndoProof rehearses the recovery plan before a database migration ships.

3
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:22,599
This is our checkout example. The release works, but its rollback quietly loses a new order.

4
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:29,701
A release contract makes the migration, application queries, and important data explicit.

5
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:35,121
Here is the problem. The rollback drops the current orders table and restores

6
00:00:35,121 --> 00:00:37,312
a snapshot taken before deployment.

7
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:43,177
This write creates order one hundred and four after deployment.

8
00:00:43,177 --> 00:00:45,823
Our recovery plan must preserve that order.

9
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:51,932
Let us rehearse. UndoProof runs real PostgreSQL through PGlite.

10
00:00:51,932 --> 00:00:54,579
The result blocks this release plan.

11
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:59,060
The forward path works. The rollback SQL also succeeds.

12
00:00:59,060 --> 00:01:03,709
But data preservation fails. Those are deliberately separate checks.

13
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:08,569
Here is the evidence. Four rows before rollback, three afterward.

14
00:01:08,569 --> 00:01:11,327
The highlighted new order has disappeared.

15
00:01:11,977 --> 00:01:16,111
We compare exact values in the supplied invariant projection.

16
00:01:16,111 --> 00:01:19,500
A successful SQL command cannot hide this failure.

17
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:24,727
This export gives Bob the exact contract and failure evidence.

18
00:01:24,727 --> 00:01:28,512
It is a manual IDE handoff for a focused repair task.

19
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:32,455
For this example, we include a repair to review.

20
00:01:32,455 --> 00:01:37,188
It keeps the compatible expanded schema when the application rolls back.

21
00:01:37,747 --> 00:01:41,700
Removing the destructive snapshot restore preserves the new data.

22
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:45,798
Apply the reviewed SQL and run the same engine again.

23
00:01:45,798 --> 00:01:48,306
This time every supplied check passes.

24
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:56,180
All four orders survive, including the write after deployment.

25
00:01:56,180 --> 00:01:58,870
The earlier application queries still work.

26
00:01:59,068 --> 00:02:03,700
Rolling back application code does not require deleting the expanded schema.

27
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:11,268
Export the tested contract, executed SQL, row comparisons and measured runtime as evidence.

28
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:17,343
History keeps both outcomes. Developers can import their own contracts, and the

29
00:02:17,343 --> 00:02:21,110
command-line runner uses the same engine as a release gate.

30
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:25,375
Here is Bob working directly in this repository.

31
00:02:25,375 --> 00:02:30,755
It added forty-one regression tests for source-derived checkout contracts, including

32
00:02:30,755 --> 00:02:33,701
the lost order and unchanged repair invariant.

33
00:02:34,351 --> 00:02:39,082
We reran the tests. The code and reviewed findings are in the repository.

34
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:48,664
UndoProof is open source. Our proposed team product adds shared policies and retained evidence.

35
00:02:49,314 --> 00:02:52,459
These results cover the tested SQL and fixtures.

36
00:02:52,459 --> 00:02:55,276
The way back belongs in the release review.
